        <div id="format" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Full Treadmill or Walking Pad?</h2>
          <p>
            Every machine above is a full-size treadmill, and for a meaningful
            group of readers that is the wrong category entirely. <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Walking pads</a>
            — the flat, deckless slabs that slide under a desk or a sofa — have
            taken over a large share of the walking market, and they are
            genuinely good at one job: accumulating steps at low speed while
            your attention is elsewhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            The honest split is this. If your goal is to be walking while you
            work, at 1.5 to 3 mph, and the machine needs to disappear when you
            are done, a walking pad is the better purchase and a full treadmill
            will mostly sit unused. If your goal is a daily walking workout —
            something with an intensity you can increase over time — the pad's
            missing incline, short deck and thin cushioning become hard limits
            within a few months. We have taken this apart properly in
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs. treadmill</a>,
            and reviewed the pads themselves in our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            A pattern worth naming: a fair number of readers end up owning
            both, and it is not as absurd as it sounds. The pad handles the
            weekday step count during working hours; the full treadmill handles
            the deliberate incline session. If you are choosing one first, buy
            for the habit you already have rather than the one you hope to
            acquire.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Walking Speed and Motor Size for Walking Only</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Most walkers use a treadmill between 2.5 and 4 mph, and a 2.0 to 2.5 CHP
            continuous motor is enough for that.</strong> Those two numbers answer most of the
            questions buyers ask once they have narrowed the field, and they explain why a
            walking-only buyer can ignore half of what a treadmill listing advertises.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start with speed. As a rough guide, easy walking for most adults sits at about 2.5 to 3
            mph, brisk walking at about 3 to 3.5 mph, and fast or power walking at about 3.5 to 4.5
            mph. Past roughly 4.5 mph, most people find walking awkward and break into a jog. Height
            moves these numbers a little, because a longer stride covers more ground at the same
            effort, which is why a tall walker's brisk pace can sit a few tenths of a mile per hour
            above a shorter walker's. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-speed-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what speed to walk on a treadmill</a>
            explains how to find your own band with the talk test in about five minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set against those bands, every top speed on this page is far more than a walker needs.
            The picks reach 10 or 12 mph where the maker or listing states it, and a walker spends
            nearly all their time in the bottom third of that range. What matters at the bottom of
            the range is different: how finely speed changes (0.1 mph steps are what you want when
            you are nudging a brisk pace up), how low the belt starts, and whether the controls sit
            where your hands already are. The Sole F65's Amazon listing gives a 0.5 to 12 mph range
            with roller controls, and the Sole F63's listing describes dial-style
            speed and incline controls built into the hand grips. Both details matter more to a
            walker than the 12 mph at the top. If a slow, steady start and secure support are the
            main concern, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking machines for seniors</a>
            ranks machines on balance and step-on safety instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            Now the motor. At walking pace, the motor is moving the belt under a modest, steady load,
            which is why walking needs far less of it than running does. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a>
            puts the walking requirement at 2.0 to 2.5 CHP (continuous horsepower) and suggests
            adding roughly 0.25 CHP for every 50 lb of user weight above 200 lb. On that rule, a
            300 lb walker would look for about 2.5 to 3.0 CHP. The continuous figure is the one to
            compare. Peak horsepower, the larger number many listings print, is what the motor
            manages for a moment, not for an hour.
          </p>
