              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F65</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5–12 mph (Amazon listing)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.25 CHP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">More than one walker needs; suits two</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Life Fitness T3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated on this page</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.0 CHP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ample, on a 400 lb frame</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Every pick with a stated continuous rating clears the walking requirement with room to
            spare, which is the point: once a motor is past about 2.5 CHP, spending more on it buys
            a walker nothing they will feel. The two exceptions to check are the T Series 5, whose
            Amazon listing does not give a motor rating at all, and the THERUN, whose 3.5 HP figure
            is not labelled. Neither is a reason to rule the machine out for walking; both are a
            reason to read the maker's specification if you are a heavier walker who will spend long
            sessions on incline, which is where a motor works hardest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Deck Length for Walking vs. Running</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>A walker under about 5 foot 10 can get by on a 55-inch deck; taller walkers,
            incline walkers and anyone who might run should buy 60 inches.</strong> Deck length is
            the specification people most often ask about after price, because it is the one you
            cannot change after delivery and the one you feel on every step.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walking and running use a deck differently. A walker always has one foot on the belt, the
            stride is short, and the body stays in roughly the same place on the deck. A runner has a
            flight phase, a longer stride and a tendency to drift forward and back as pace changes,
            so runners are usually told to look for 60 inches as a minimum. Walking narrows that need,
            but incline widens it again: as the deck rises, most walkers lengthen their step and
            drift toward the front of the belt, so a 55-inch deck that feels fine on the flat can feel
            short at 10%. Width matters much less for walkers, who track straight; 20 inches suits
            nearly everyone.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How you will use it</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Deck length to look for</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Picks on this page that fit</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
