      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">14. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill for walking only?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Sole F63 is our overall pick for walking-only use. It pairs
                a full 20 x 60-inch deck with cushioning designed for long
                daily walks, and it requires no
                subscription. If your budget stops around $600, the NordicTrack
                T Series 5 gives up the least that a walker will actually
                feel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a strong motor if you only walk on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Walking at 2.5 to 4 mph asks very little of a treadmill
                motor, so anything rated 2.5 CHP continuous duty or above has
                ample headroom. Motor size is the wrong spec to optimise for as
                a walker. Deck length, cushioning quality and incline range
                change your daily experience far more, and they are where the
                money is better spent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad good enough, or do I need a full treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A walking pad is good enough if your goal is accumulating
                low-intensity steps while you work, at speeds below about 3.5
                mph. It is not good enough if you want incline training, a
                comfortable stride at higher walking speeds, or a machine that
                lasts a decade. Most walking pads have short decks, minimal
                cushioning and no incline, which caps how far the habit can
                progress.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should the deck be on a walking treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for 55 inches minimum, and 60 inches if you are over about
                5 foot 10 or plan to walk on incline. Stride length grows as
                incline increases, so a deck that feels fine on the flat can
                feel short at 10% grade. Deck width matters less for walkers
                than for runners; 20 inches is comfortable for nearly everyone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much incline do I need for walking workouts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ten to twelve per cent covers the great majority of
                incline-walking sessions, including the popular 12-3-30
                protocol. Going beyond that only makes sense if you
                specifically intend to train at steep grades, in which case an
                incline trainer reaching 40% gives walkers a progression path
                that does not require increasing speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are walking treadmills quiet enough for an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Walking is the quietest thing you can do on a treadmill, but
                the noise your neighbours hear is mostly impact transmitted
                through the floor rather than motor sound through the air. A
                heavier frame, a well-cushioned deck and a proper rubber mat
                under the machine do more for that than any advertised decibel
                figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good walking speed on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most adults, easy walking is about 2.5 to 3 mph, brisk walking about 3 to 3.5 mph and fast walking about 3.5 to 4.5 mph. Above roughly 4.5 mph most people break into a jog, so a walker uses only the bottom third of a 10 or 12 mph treadmill. Once your brisk pace stops feeling like work, add incline rather than speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 55-inch treadmill long enough for walking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walkers under about 5 foot 10 on the flat, usually yes. If you are taller, or plan to spend much of your time on incline, where the stride lengthens, a 60-inch deck is the safer choice. On this page the NordicTrack T Series 5 and the THERUN have 55-inch belts; the Sole F63, Sole F65, ProForm Carbon TLX and Life Fitness T3 have 60-inch decks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run on a walking treadmill later?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the full-size picks here, occasional jogging is fine: every pick whose top speed we list reaches 10 to 12 mph, and a 60-inch deck is the length runners are usually told to look for. For regular running the motor starts to matter: about 2.75 to 3.0 CHP continuous for jogging a few times a week and 3.5 CHP or more for daily running. A walking pad is a different machine, and most are not built for running.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Walkers Make When Buying</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Nearly every regretful purchase we hear about traces back to one of these four. All of them come from applying a runner's buying logic to a walker's use case.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for motor headroom instead of deck length</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 3.5 CHP motor and a 2.75 CHP motor are indistinguishable at 3 mph. A 60-inch deck and a 55-inch deck are not. Buyers routinely spend an extra $300 on the motor spec and then accept the shorter deck, which is the one thing they will notice every single session. Rank deck length first and treat anything above 2.5 CHP as sufficient.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the incline ceiling until after delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Incline is the walker's only real progression lever, and plenty of budget machines stop at 10%. If you have a specific protocol in mind — 12-3-30 being the common one — check the maximum grade before you buy, not after. Discovering your machine cannot reach the number the workout requires is a frustrating and entirely avoidable outcome.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging cushioning in the first week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Deck cushioning firms up as the elastomers bed in, and several machines that feel plush on delivery are noticeably harder a month later. For someone walking an hour a day that is the difference between comfort and heel pain. Read long-term owner reports rather than launch reviews, and treat a suspiciously soft out-of-the-box feel as a question rather than a selling point.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the room you wish you walked in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A better treadmill in the basement loses to a decent treadmill in the living room every time, because the one you walk past is the one you use. Decide where the machine will live before you decide which machine it is, then measure that space including ceiling height at full incline. Footprint and placement drive long-term use far more than any spec on the box.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

